Now I understand the format. The input is an ini file with sections like `[jobname]` and options like `description=$ncpus`. The last byte of the input is the `type` passed to `parse_jobs_ini`.

Let me analyze the bug in `fio_keyword_replace`:

```c
while ((s = strstr(opt, kw->word)) != NULL) {
        char *new = calloc(strlen(opt) + 1, 1);
        char *o_org = opt;
        int olen = s - opt;
        int len;

        memcpy(new, opt, olen);
        len = sprintf(new + olen, "%s", kw->replace);

        opt += strlen(kw->word) + olen;
        /* keeps final zero thanks to calloc */
        if (strlen(opt))
                memcpy(new + olen + len, opt, opt - o_org - 1);

        opt = new;
        free(o_org);
        docalc = 1;
}
```

The bug: `memcpy(new + olen + len, opt, opt - o_org - 1)` copies `opt - o_org - 1` bytes. But `opt - o_org = strlen(kw->word) + olen`, so it copies `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1` bytes from `opt`. The remaining string at `opt` has length `strlen(opt)`. If `strlen(opt) < strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1`, then memcpy reads beyond the buffer — a heap-buffer-overflow **read**.

Wait, but `opt` points into the original buffer `o_org` which has length `strlen(o_org)`. `opt` is at offset `strlen(kw->word) + olen` from `o_org`. The bytes available from `opt` to the end of the buffer is `strlen(o_org) - (strlen(kw->word) + olen)`. The memcpy reads `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1` bytes. So overflow read happens when `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1 > strlen(o_org) - (strlen(kw->word) + olen)`, i.e., `2*(strlen(kw->word) + olen) - 1 > strlen(o_org)`.

Hmm, but actually the more direct issue: the memcpy copies `opt - o_org - 1` bytes from `opt`. Since `opt` is at offset `strlen(kw->word)+olen` and the buffer is `strlen(o_org)` long, the read of `opt - o_org - 1` bytes from `opt` overflows when `opt - o_org - 1 > strlen(o_org) - (opt - o_org)`, i.e. `2*(opt-o_org) - 1 > strlen(o_org)`.

Actually wait — let me reconsider. The intent was to copy the remaining string after the keyword, which is `strlen(opt)` bytes. But it copies `opt - o_org - 1` bytes instead. If `opt - o_org - 1 > strlen(opt)`, that's a heap-buffer-overflow read.

`opt - o_org - 1 = strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1`. And `strlen(opt) = strlen(o_org) - (strlen(kw->word) + olen)`.

So overflow when `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1 > strlen(o_org) - strlen(kw->word) - olen`, i.e., `2*(strlen(kw->word)+olen) - 1 > strlen(o_org)`.

For `$ncpus` (6 chars), with `description=$ncpus` (17 chars): `olen = 12` (position of `$ncpus`), `strlen(kw->word)=6`. So `opt - o_org - 1 = 6+12-1 = 17`. `strlen(opt) = 17 - 17 = 0`. So memcpy reads 17 bytes from `opt` which points at the null terminator — reading 17 bytes past the end of the 17-byte buffer (well, the buffer is `strlen+1 = 18` bytes including null). `opt` points at index 17 (the null). Reading 17 bytes from index 17 = reading indices 17..33, but buffer is 18 bytes (0..17). So overflow read of 16 bytes past the end.

So the trigger is simply `description=$ncpus` (or any option with a keyword where the keyword is at the end). The description says exactly: "triggered when parsing options such as 'description=$ncpus'".

